  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRidging overhead with warm southwest return flow today.\nTemperatures will likely climb well into low 70s to possibly mid\n70s today. Thursday will be another warm day, but a frontal\nsystem crosses the area and drops temperatures a few degrees\nwith widespread rain showers. Temperatures will cool each day \nfor Friday through Sunday, first behind the cold front later \nThursday, then with another cold front and sfc low crossing the \narea on Saturday. With the cooling temperatures in the 50s, \nafternoon highs will be closer to normal for Sunday and Monday, \nbut still slightly above for early November. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front will push through the region on Thursday, bringing \nthe widespread showers. Most of the shower activity will occur \nduring the early to mid morning hours, amounting to a tenth to \nquarter of an inch of rainfall. The sfc low will slowly track \nacross the Great Lakes and to New England from late Wednesday \nthrough Sunday, becoming vertically stacked with its mid and \nupper level lows. This will result in a prolonged period where \nshowers will be possible at times within the cyclonic flow for \nmost of the area. Behind the passing cold front on Thursday, a\ndry slot moves into the region, providing a break in the \nshowers. Most of the area should stay dry Friday, with possibly\nshowers continuing across the Poconos and southern Catskills as\na wave of low pressure tracks along the Mid Atlantic Coast. \nShower potential will increase again Saturday morning from west \nto east, as the upper level low tracks into the area and slowly \npushes through, with showers lingering until Sunday morning. \n\nAnother trough dropping southeast out of Canada will bring \nanother chance for showers late Sunday night into Monday, with \nlake enhancement adding to the shower potential.",
